1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,777
Clip an inductance meter across two
terminals of a motor,

2
00:00:03,817 --> 00:00:06,096
and turn the shaft slowly by hand.

3
00:00:06,136 --> 00:00:13,454
With surface magnets, the reading hardly
moves: 0.085 mH in Lesson 5's 2D model.

4
00:00:13,494 --> 00:00:16,570
Bury the magnets inside the iron,

5
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:23,108
and it swings from 0.124 to 0.188 mH,
twice per electrical cycle.

6
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:26,649
That swing is saliency: the rotor isn't
magnetically round.

7
00:00:26,689 --> 00:00:30,009
And it gives the motor a second kind of
torque.

8
00:00:31,009 --> 00:00:33,396
First, the answers from Lesson 9.

9
00:00:33,436 --> 00:00:39,627
One: 9 slots and 6 poles cog 18 times per
revolution, their least common multiple.

10
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:47,940
Two: 15 A on the q axis gives 0.039 × 15 =
0.585 N·m.

11
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:51,647
Three: yes, when Lq is larger than Ld.

12
00:00:51,687 --> 00:00:57,259
Then (Ld − Lq) i_d i_q is positive for
negative i_d.

13
00:00:58,159 --> 00:01:02,223
Start with the surface magnets. To the
stator's current,

14
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:06,626
a magnet is almost air: its recoil
permeability is 1.05.

15
00:01:06,666 --> 00:01:12,648
So flux on the d axis crosses the gap and
3 mm of magnet; on the q axis,

16
00:01:12,688 --> 00:01:18,552
between the magnets, the gap and 3 mm of
air. Almost the same path.

17
00:01:18,592 --> 00:01:25,100
The solver agrees. Turn the rotor through
a whole electrical cycle:

18
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:29,895
Ld and Lq both stay at 0.0423 mH, within
0.1%.

19
00:01:29,935 --> 00:01:33,292
The nominal 0.05 mH adds the end windings,

20
00:01:33,332 --> 00:01:38,928
which a 2D model can't see. Either way,
this rotor looks round.

21
00:01:39,828 --> 00:01:46,094
Now bury them. Each pole gets a flat
magnet, 9 by 3 mm, 4 mm under the surface,

22
00:01:46,134 --> 00:01:51,263
an air pocket at each end, and a 1 mm iron
rib between poles.

23
00:01:51,303 --> 00:01:57,594
Same stator, same 6 turns per coil. The
magnets give λm = 4.13 mWb,

24
00:01:57,634 --> 00:02:03,942
against 5.94 for the surface rotor, with
70% of its magnet area.

25
00:02:03,982 --> 00:02:07,367
A real rotor closes each pocket with a
thin iron bridge,

26
00:02:07,407 --> 00:02:09,218
which the magnet saturates.

27
00:02:09,258 --> 00:02:13,522
Linear iron can't saturate, so here the
pockets stay open.

28
00:02:13,562 --> 00:02:19,331
Two saturated 0.5 mm bridges would leak
about 19% of the magnet's flux.

29
00:02:20,231 --> 00:02:24,406
Now drive 20 A along each axis, with the
magnets switched off.

30
00:02:24,446 --> 00:02:29,852
On the d axis, the flux has to cross the
magnet: 3 mm of near-air. On the q axis,

31
00:02:29,892 --> 00:02:35,531
it runs between the magnets, through iron,
and crosses little more than the gap.

32
00:02:35,571 --> 00:02:46,252
The solver gives Ld = 0.0605 mH and Lq =
0.0933 mH: Lq is 1.54 times Ld.

33
00:02:46,292 --> 00:02:51,175
Ld is above the surface rotor's, because
some d-axis flux bypasses the magnet:

34
00:02:51,215 --> 00:02:55,578
through the iron above it, across the
pockets, and down the ribs.

35
00:02:55,618 --> 00:03:03,704
The series' reference IPM is a separate
design, with more turns and end windings:

36
00:03:03,744 --> 00:03:10,564
Ld = 0.16, Lq = 0.32 mH, λm = 5.0 mWb.
Later lessons use those.

37
00:03:11,464 --> 00:03:14,259
Back to the meter. Here's the self-
inductance

38
00:03:14,299 --> 00:03:17,331
of phase a as the buried-magnet rotor
turns.

39
00:03:17,371 --> 00:03:19,856
It's lowest when a magnet faces the coil,

40
00:03:19,896 --> 00:03:23,605
and highest a quarter cycle later, when
the q axis does.

41
00:03:23,645 --> 00:03:27,943
A north pole and a south pole look the
same to the iron,

42
00:03:27,983 --> 00:03:32,659
so the pattern repeats every half cycle:
L0 + L2 cos 2θe.

43
00:03:32,699 --> 00:03:36,023
The mutual inductances swing the same way.

44
00:03:36,063 --> 00:03:42,571
Park's transform turns it all into two
near-constants, with Ld − Lq = 3 L2.

45
00:03:43,471 --> 00:03:48,818
Lesson 8's co-energy gives the torque.
With the magnets off, it's ½ iᵀ L i,

46
00:03:48,858 --> 00:03:53,499
and torque is its slope with rotor angle
at constant current.

47
00:03:53,539 --> 00:03:57,933
Put the cos 2θ terms into all three
phases,

48
00:03:57,973 --> 00:04:03,286
add them up, and you get 3/2 p (Ld − Lq)
i_d i_q.

49
00:04:03,326 --> 00:04:07,328
Physically, the iron turns to bring its
easy path, the q axis,

50
00:04:07,368 --> 00:04:11,605
toward the stator field, like Lesson 8's
reluctance actuator.

51
00:04:11,645 --> 00:04:19,458
Switch the magnets off in the solver and
sweep the current angle at 20 A:

52
00:04:19,498 --> 00:04:26,428
a pure sin 2γ, 0.039 N·m at 135°, within
0.04% of the formula.

53
00:04:26,468 --> 00:04:32,038
It's positive only between 90° and 180°,
where i_d is negative.

54
00:04:32,938 --> 00:04:37,401
Now switch the magnets back on, and
compare both rotors at 20 A.

55
00:04:37,441 --> 00:04:44,737
The surface rotor gives a pure sine: 0.713
N·m at 90°, all current on q.

56
00:04:44,777 --> 00:04:51,698
The buried-magnet rotor adds its sin 2γ
term, so its peak moves past 90°,

57
00:04:51,738 --> 00:04:56,957
to about 99°, with a little negative i_d:
0.502 N·m.

58
00:04:56,997 --> 00:05:01,544
That's only 1.2% more than pure q current.

59
00:05:01,584 --> 00:05:10,384
Here (Lq − Ld) times the current is small
next to λm: 0.66 against 4.13 mWb.

60
00:05:11,284 --> 00:05:18,442
In the reference IPM, it's 3.2 against 5.0
mWb, and the reluctance term matters.

61
00:05:18,482 --> 00:05:26,670
20 A, all on q, makes 0.600 N·m. Turn the
current to 114.7°, i_d = −8.3

62
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:35,197
A and i_q = 18.2 A, and it makes 0.691:
15% more from the same current.

63
00:05:35,237 --> 00:05:40,059
Choosing that angle at every current is
maximum torque per amp, MTPA.

64
00:05:40,099 --> 00:05:45,672
The angle grows with current; in Lesson
20, it goes into the controller.

65
00:05:46,584 --> 00:05:49,765
You can measure Ld and Lq with an LCR
meter.

66
00:05:49,805 --> 00:05:54,385
Connect it across two terminals, a and b,
and leave c open.

67
00:05:54,425 --> 00:05:58,503
Choose series inductance at a low
frequency, say 100 Hz.

68
00:05:58,543 --> 00:06:04,539
Turn the rotor 1.5 mechanical degrees at a
time, and hold it for each reading.

69
00:06:04,579 --> 00:06:12,695
With a star winding, the reading is (Ld +
Lq) + (Ld − Lq) cos(2θe + 60°).

70
00:06:12,735 --> 00:06:17,240
Its minimum is 2 Ld, its maximum 2 Lq.

71
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:23,643
No neutral needed: halve the extremes. For
the reference IPM,

72
00:06:23,683 --> 00:06:30,870
expect 0.32 to 0.64 mH; for the surface-
magnet motor, a flat 0.10.

73
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:34,318
Checked on the solver's 2D rotor:

74
00:06:34,358 --> 00:06:42,591
half its extremes give 0.0621 and 0.0938
mH, within 3% of the Park values.

75
00:06:43,491 --> 00:06:46,097
Careful, though. The meter drives
milliamps,

76
00:06:46,137 --> 00:06:50,666
so it reads the small-signal, incremental
inductance near zero current.

77
00:06:50,706 --> 00:06:55,626
At rated current, the iron on the q path
saturates and Lq falls. The magnets

78
00:06:55,666 --> 00:07:01,148
already saturate the bridges, which shifts
Ld. A linear model can't show either.

79
00:07:01,188 --> 00:07:09,274
Frequency matters too: eddy currents push
flux out of the steel. With μr = 4000,

80
00:07:09,314 --> 00:07:17,395
the skin depth is 0.57 mm at 100 Hz, but
0.18 mm at 1 kHz, half a lamination.

81
00:07:17,435 --> 00:07:20,774
So read at two frequencies; if they
disagree, go lower.

82
00:07:20,814 --> 00:07:26,230
And disconnect the drive: anything on the
third terminal lets current couple in.

83
00:07:27,130 --> 00:07:29,410
Three predictions before next time.

84
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:31,355
One: an 8-pole IPM motor.

85
00:07:31,395 --> 00:07:36,018
How many inductance cycles per mechanical
revolution?

86
00:07:36,918 --> 00:07:43,534
Two: the reference IPM, with i_d = −10 A
and i_q = 10 A. What torque?

87
00:07:44,434 --> 00:07:49,808
Three: what can bias an LCR-meter
inductance reading on a stationary rotor?

88
00:07:50,708 --> 00:07:55,599
Next time: space vectors, three phase
currents as one rotating arrow.
